WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.550
Let's start with a conversation many teams recognize. A

2
00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.470
partner says, we keep giving you feedback, but nothing

3
00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:10.430
happens. You're the supervisor receiving that message. Where do

4
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:10.810
you begin?

5
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.200
I'd be tempted to list all the meetings we've

6
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.160
held. But that wouldn't answer what happened to their

7
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.420
feedback. I'd ask which concern they want us to

8
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.380
follow up on.

9
00:00:21.883 --> 00:00:25.243
Right. Inviting people to a meeting and responding to

10
00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:29.203
their input are different things. Before we talk, I'd

11
00:00:29.223 --> 00:00:32.003
check the comments, the decision, and what can still

12
00:00:32.023 --> 00:00:32.383
change.

13
00:00:32.893 --> 00:00:35.533
And who can change it. I don't want to

14
00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:38.333
offer something beyond my authority just to make the

15
00:00:38.353 --> 00:00:39.553
conversation easier.

16
00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:43.653
The partner explains that they asked for a clearer

17
00:00:43.673 --> 00:00:47.333
contact route in a service guide. The final guide

18
00:00:47.353 --> 00:00:50.133
still has the old wording, and they never heard

19
00:00:50.153 --> 00:00:52.453
why. How could you check your understanding?

20
00:00:53.174 --> 00:00:55.874
I'd say, you want to know how that comment

21
00:00:55.894 --> 00:00:59.994
was considered, and you haven't received an explanation. Have

22
00:01:00.014 --> 00:01:02.294
I got that right? Then I'd give them room

23
00:01:02.314 --> 00:01:03.314
to correct me.

24
00:01:03.837 --> 00:01:07.137
That last part is important. A summary is an

25
00:01:07.157 --> 00:01:10.437
invitation to clarify. If they correct it, we need

26
00:01:10.457 --> 00:01:12.957
to use the correction, rather than return to the

27
00:01:12.977 --> 00:01:14.277
defense we'd already prepared.

28
00:01:15.247 --> 00:01:17.887
What about speaking with the colleague who handled the

29
00:01:17.907 --> 00:01:20.867
comments? We still need to discuss responsibility.

30
00:01:21.565 --> 00:01:24.865
We do. Start with what you can establish. The

31
00:01:24.885 --> 00:01:27.885
reviewer saw the comments after approval. Ask what happened

32
00:01:27.905 --> 00:01:31.545
between receipt and forwarding. Was the expected handoff clear?

33
00:01:32.065 --> 00:01:33.745
Then discuss the action and its effect.

34
00:01:34.335 --> 00:01:36.955
That gives the colleague a chance to explain without

35
00:01:36.975 --> 00:01:40.195
turning the conversation into a judgment about their character.

36
00:01:40.575 --> 00:01:43.455
If the expectation was clear and it wasn't met,

37
00:01:43.675 --> 00:01:45.515
we can address that directly too.

38
00:01:46.744 --> 00:01:49.984
Now the team disagrees. One person wants to hold

39
00:01:50.004 --> 00:01:52.904
the whole guide. Another says we must release it

40
00:01:52.924 --> 00:01:55.504
today. How do you keep the conversation useful?

41
00:01:56.202 --> 00:01:59.422
I'd bring it back to the particular instruction. What

42
00:01:59.442 --> 00:02:02.242
could happen if it stays unclear? Can the responsible

43
00:02:02.262 --> 00:02:05.402
team confirm the correction today? What needs an approval

44
00:02:05.422 --> 00:02:08.442
decision? That gives us something more useful than arguing

45
00:02:08.462 --> 00:02:09.662
about who cares most.

46
00:02:10.249 --> 00:02:13.209
And we can acknowledge the pressure. People may have

47
00:02:13.229 --> 00:02:17.149
worked hard against a deadline. That effort matters, and

48
00:02:17.169 --> 00:02:19.429
so does the person trying to use the guide.

49
00:02:20.336 --> 00:02:23.376
Suppose we did miss the comment. I'd want to

50
00:02:23.396 --> 00:02:26.476
say that plainly: we missed your concern, and you

51
00:02:26.496 --> 00:02:29.356
should have received an answer. Here's what we're doing

52
00:02:29.376 --> 00:02:29.836
now.

53
00:02:30.737 --> 00:02:34.577
Yes. A direct acknowledgment doesn't need a long explanation

54
00:02:34.597 --> 00:02:37.517
of our good intentions. Follow it with a correction

55
00:02:37.537 --> 00:02:39.917
you can make, or the route for a decision.

56
00:02:40.457 --> 00:02:42.857
The partner doesn't have to reassure us before we

57
00:02:42.877 --> 00:02:43.237
help.

58
00:02:43.875 --> 00:02:47.315
They might still be disappointed. We can respect that

59
00:02:47.335 --> 00:02:50.035
and keep doing the work. We shouldn't ask for

60
00:02:50.055 --> 00:02:52.415
forgiveness as a condition of moving forward.

61
00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:56.746
What would you change before the next review?

62
00:02:57.364 --> 00:03:00.324
I'd name who responds to comments and when, allow

63
00:03:00.344 --> 00:03:03.484
written input as well as discussion, and explain the

64
00:03:03.504 --> 00:03:06.564
final decision. A short follow-up can say what changed,

65
00:03:06.784 --> 00:03:07.844
what didn't, and why.

66
00:03:08.581 --> 00:03:11.821
For your own practice, choose one conversation coming up.

67
00:03:12.361 --> 00:03:14.821
Write an opening question that helps you understand the

68
00:03:14.841 --> 00:03:18.261
concern. Then name one commitment you can actually keep.

69
00:03:18.601 --> 00:03:20.581
You can pause and rehearse it in your own

70
00:03:20.601 --> 00:03:20.981
words.

71
00:03:22.899 --> 00:03:26.099
And after the conversation, ask yourself: can the other

72
00:03:26.119 --> 00:03:29.159
person tell what happened to their concern? That question

73
00:03:29.179 --> 00:03:31.779
can help staff and leaders turn a difficult exchange

74
00:03:31.799 --> 00:03:33.839
into useful guidance and dependable follow-through.
